  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Significant precipitation is expected Tuesday \nand Tuesday night, with light wintry precipitation possible over\nnorth/northwest areas. The wintry precipitation is not expected\nto impact travel, and flooding is not expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure across the Great Lakes will draw a warm front \ntoward the region Monday night through early Tuesday. \nOverrunning precipitation in advance of the warm front will \ndevelop across the region during the day Tuesday. Precipitation\nshould generally be in the form of rain. However, dependent on \nthe onset time, just enough cold air could across northern \nareas and the Central Highlands should support an interval of \nsnow or a rain/snow mix during the day Tuesday. This will \nmainly be from Presque Isle and Greenville north. Accumulations \nwill be minimal for most places if there is any at all and \ntravel impacts are not anticipated. There could be up to 3 \ninches mainly at higher elevations of the North Woods.\nTemperatures slowly warm into Tuesday night and we anticipate\nall areas changing to rain.\n\nFairly significant precipitation is expected areawide, with\ngenerally 0.5 to 1.25 inches of total precipitation. Flooding is\nnot expected, but this will help with drought conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Light rain possible Downeast tonight and light \nrain likely in the north Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nThe remnants of Isaias track south of the Gulf of Maine tonight.\nThe northern edge of the precipitation shield should clip \nDowneast areas tonight, but totals look quite light with one\ntenth of an inch or less, and mainly south of Bangor. Then\nduring the day Monday, a cold front will then cross the area \nfrom north to south with light rain likely mainly north of\nBangor for a few hours as the front moves through. Totals will\nbe around a tenth of an inch. Temperatures will fall behind the\ncold front, with readings falling into the 40s in the north in\nthe afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Widespread rain is likely again Friday/Friday\nnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nAn active weather pattern will remain, with another low pressure\nsystem passing through the area from west to east around \nFriday/Friday night. This is still a lot of uncertainty, but it \ndoes appear very likely most places will get at least 0.25 \ninches of precipitation, with some places exceeding 0.50 inches.\nWinds and wintry precipitation are both possibilities depending\non the track of the system, but are not likelihoods at this \npoint...just something to keep an eye on.",
